+/// What the suite caught among a table of mutants: one walk per mutant, per invariant.
+///
+/// This is the whole of mutation testing, and it is three lines: a mutant is a *model* somebody
+/// changed ([`Model`], `jobq/3.19-property-invariants`), the suite is the invariants it has, and a
+/// mutant is killed when one of them comes back with a counter-example. Everything a tool adds on
+/// top of that is bookkeeping — where to apply the change, whether it builds, how long to wait —
+/// and the bookkeeping is why the tool is worth having.
+///
+/// One seed for the whole run, because that is what makes two runs comparable: a suite that caught a
+/// mutant under one seed and missed it under another is a suite whose score depends on the weather.
+/// A mutant that no invariant catches is a *survivor*, and naming it is the caller's business: this
+/// is the arithmetic of the run, and the names of the rules are the table that was planted.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn caught(
+    seed: u64,
+    length: usize,
+    invariants: &[&dyn Invariant],
+    mutants: &[&dyn Model],
+) -> Score {
+    todo!("jobq/3.20-mutation-threshold: one walk per mutant, per invariant")
+}
